=============================================================================
TURBO/64 BBS (T/64) v__VERSION__
A Commodore 64 BBS for the Oscar64 compiler
=============================================================================

Note: This project is under active development and is not yet
feature-complete.

WHAT THIS IS
------------

TURBO/64 BBS is a Commodore 64 BBS written in C for the Oscar64 compiler.
It targets native .prg output for real hardware (including C64 Ultimate)
and VICE emulation.

Current status: v__VERSION__ -- login/registration, terminal translation
(PETSCII, ANSI/CP437, ASCII), bulletin boards, door programs (external
Oscar64 plug-ins, with an author dev kit), and the Configure editor are
working.

Not working: private mail, file transfers, SysOp chat, polls/voting,
and more remain stubbed.

REQUIREMENTS
------------

Hardware / emulator:
- Commodore 64 with SwiftLink/ACIA cartridge at $DE00, OR C64 Ultimate
  with built-in ACIA, OR VICE x64sc with tcpser modem bridge
- Two .D81 disk images -- one for the BBS, one blank for message boards
  (device 9)
- 16 MB REU required for message boards and some other features.  The
  C64 Ultimate has one built in; VICE can be configured with one.  The
  BBS boots and lets you log in without a REU, but reading and posting
  messages need it.

Developer build host (macOS / Linux):
- oscar64 compiler -- bash tools/install-oscar64.sh
- c1541 from VICE package (disk assembly)
- c64u deploy tool for C64U -- bash tools/install-c64u.sh
- tcpser for VICE modem emulation -- macOS: brew install tcpser

QUICK START
-----------

1. Mount TURBO64-__VERSION__.d81 on device 8.

2. Initialize the files (first time only):
   LOAD "CONFIGURE-__VERSION__",8
   RUN
   Choose I -- INIT FILES from the main menu.
   This creates USR LOG, USR PROF, and CALLERS.
   Record 1 is always the SysOp account: handle SYSOP, password PASS,
   access level 5.  Change it!

3. Configure the BBS:
   From CONFIGURE, choose C -- CONFIG to edit settings, devices, modem
   type, and baud rate.  Changes are saved to the config SEQ file
   immediately.

4. Set up message boards disk (device 9):
   Format a blank .D81, mount it on device 9.  In CONFIGURE, choose
   M -- MSG AREAS then C -- CREATE to add boards.

5. Run the BBS:
   LOAD "BOOT-__VERSION__",8
   RUN

   Expected startup:
     TURBO/64 BBS V__VERSION__
     LOADING SETUP...
       BBS: <your bbs name>
       SYSOP: <your name>
     INIT MODEM...
       ACIA STATUS: $10
     CHECKING REU...
       REU: 16 MB
     CHECKING USR LOG...
       USR LOG: OK

   If USR LOG: NOT FOUND or USR LOG: EMPTY, return to CONFIGURE and
   run Init Files.

CONFIGURATION REFERENCE
-----------------------

data/config default values:

  BBS_NAME         A NEW T/64 BBS
  BBS_CITY         SOMEWHERE, CA
  SYSOP_NAME       JOE SYSOP
  NEW_USER_LEVEL   1
  MIN_CALL_TIME    5
  MAX_CALL_TIME    60
  DEV_SYSTEM       8
  DEV_MSGS         9
  DEV_FILES        8
  DEV_DOORS        8
  MODEM_INIT       ATZ
  BAUD_RATE        38400
  MODEM_TIMEOUT    255
  ALLOW_NEW_USERS  1
  ALLOW_UPLOADS    1

Device values can include a drive number and CBM DOS init string:
8, 8;0:, or 8;0:;CD:BBS.  The BBS sends the init string to the drive
at startup.

ACCESS LEVELS
-------------

  Level  Name      Description
  ------ --------- ---------------------------------------------------
  0      Deleted   Banned / deleted account
  1      New       Unvalidated new user (default for registrants)
  2      User      Standard validated user
  3      Power     Power user
  4      Co-SysOp  Co-SysOp
  5      SysOp     Full access; cannot be deleted

Each level has daily limits and capability flags edited in CONFIGURE:
  CALLS/DAY  -- max logins per day for the level
  MINS/DAY   -- max online minutes per day (enforced live)
  FLAGS      -- capability bits (A=anon, P=page, S=send mail,
                J=join polls, U=upload, T=no time limit,
                C=no call limit, Y=sysop)

WHAT IS NOT YET IMPLEMENTED
----------------------------

The following features return a placeholder message:

- Private mail
- File transfers (upload/download)
- SysOp chat / page
- Polls and votes
- Last caller display
- System info
- Graffiti wall
- Preferences (can't edit)
- Help
- Local chat
- 80-column (ANSI) message viewer
- WFC F keys (local login, log viewer, etc.)
- Spy F keys (chat, change access level)
- Adapt message area to 80 cols

Data structures and CONFIGURE admin modules for all of the above are
in place.

HOW TO CONTRIBUTE
-----------------

TURBO/64 is an active project and contributions of all kinds are welcome
-- you do not need to be a C programmer to help.

Developers: the stubbed features listed above are the priority.  Each one
has its data layer and CONFIGURE admin UI in place; what is missing is
the caller-facing session logic inside src/features/.  If you want to
pick up a feature, open an issue first to avoid duplication.  Good entry
points: src/features/mail.c, src/features/vote.c,
src/features/callers.c -- all thin stubs waiting to be wired up on the
same pattern as src/features/bulletin.c.

Artists and SysOps: the BBS ships with bare-bones gfiles.  Needed:
PETSCII art (mode 0), ANSI/CP437 art (mode 1), ASCII variants (mode 2),
and menu prompt files (p.*).  Files live in data/gfiles/ and are
assembled directly onto the disk image by make disk.

Testers: real-hardware and emulator coverage is critical.  Specifically
needed: SD2IEC (REL file edge cases), VICE regression testing, real
1541/1571/1581 drives, and terminal clients (SyncTerm, PuTTY, netcat,
real C64 terminals).

File issues on GitHub with hardware/firmware versions, a description of
what you did, and the exact error or unexpected behaviour.

